      "text": "To solve the problem, we will use the concept of conditional probability. The probability that the first card drawn is a 6 and the second card is a Queen can be calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Calculate the probability of drawing a 6 first:**\n   There are 4 sixes in a standard deck of 52 cards. Therefore, the probability of drawing a 6 first is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{52} = \\frac{1}{13}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the probability of drawing a Queen second, given that a 6 has already been drawn:**\n   If a 6 is drawn first, there are now 51 cards left in the deck. There are 4 Queens in the deck initially, and if a 6 is drawn first, one of those Queens will still be left, but there are now 12 Queens left in the deck since one Queen is already counted among the sixes. Therefore, the probability of drawing a Queen second, given that a 6 has already been drawn, is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{51}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the combined probability:**\n   The combined probability of both events happening (drawing a 6 first and then a Queen) is the product of the individual probabilities:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{First card is a 6 and second card is a Queen}) = P(\\text{First card is a 6}) \\times P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{1}{13} \\times \\frac{4}{51} = \\frac{4}{663}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first card is a 6 and the second card is a Queen is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{663}}\\).",
